1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They have saved millions of lives.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Tiny pills that have the power to kill infection.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
But are the days of the antibiotic miracle at an end?

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, the soaring successes and unexpected failures of antibiotics,

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
the wonder drugs on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The End

7
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:51,280
1941 was the first year that penicillin was used

8
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,820
to save a human life.

9
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,700
Since that time, antibiotics have transformed the world.

10
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:02,140
Scientists estimate that as a class of drugs,

11
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:06,580
antibiotics have helped add 10 years to the life expectancy

12
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:12,680
of every man, woman, and child on this planet.

13
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,260
Hailed as wonder drugs because they killed infection

14
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,020
without causing harmful side effects,

15
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:21,660
they have become the most widely prescribed

16
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,660
of all medications.

17
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,060
Every year in the United States alone,

18
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:31,140
more than 150 million prescriptions are written,

19
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:35,400
antibiotics seem to be the answer to an age-old quest.

20
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,380
From the earliest times, healers

21
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:47,840
searched for substances in nature that were medicinal.

22
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:50,220
They used a wide variety of remedies,

23
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:54,260
including herbs, tree bark, and plant resins.

24
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:56,760
According to early botanical sources,

25
00:01:56,761 --> 00:02:01,300
These had quite complex effects on the body.

26
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:07,880
Nicotine, opium, and cocaine had clear effects on the body.

27
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:11,380
There was ipecac and Peruvian bark.

28
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:12,740
And there were a number of others.

29
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:16,340
The willow tree was the source of the development

30
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:17,880
of aspirin products.

31
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:23,320
Scientists have even found evidence

32
00:02:23,321 --> 00:02:26,480
that thousands of years ago, the Egyptians

33
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:29,820
wrote down remedies for common infections

34
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:35,600
and actually used the antibiotic tetracycline.

35
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,200
There are ancient mummies that have been looked at,

36
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,300
and they found a fluorescent material in their bones, which

37
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:47,080
turned out to be tetracycline.

38
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:51,080
The question is whether or not they purposely

39
00:02:51,081 --> 00:02:53,780
took tetracycline, which I doubt.

40
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:58,220
Rather, they were probably ingesting the organism.

41
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:03,220
And maybe, just maybe, there was some historic evidence

42
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:06,860
in their life that eating this particular substance

43
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,760
would help them.

44
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:12,760
Well into the 19th century, mankind

45
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:17,400
had little or no understanding of the causes of disease.

46
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,600
In general, people believe that illness

47
00:03:19,601 --> 00:03:22,060
was caused by a failure of the body.

48
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:28,080
The most prevalent theory of disease

49
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:31,380
was that of the four humors, which had been around

50
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:33,440
since ancient times.

51
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:38,120
The four humors were blood, phlegm, yellow bile,

52
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:39,820
and black bile.

53
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:43,060
And the idea was it was the balance of these humors

54
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:48,020
that determined whether you were sick or healthy.

55
00:03:48,021 --> 00:03:50,960
The lack of understanding about what caused disease

56
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:55,260
made any sort of effective treatment impossible.

57
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:58,260
Doctors had very little, if anything,

58
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:02,900
to really treat patients with to alter the course of a disease.

59
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,500
They had prescriptions they could write.

60
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:07,700
The more popular prescriptions had more alcohol

61
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,000
than other prescriptions.

62
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,480
And they were always written in Latin,

63
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,920
and they were sort of secretive.

64
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:18,020
But they were basically palliatives and placebos.

65
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:22,020
Stories abound of famous people whose lives were cut short

66
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:27,660
by simple infections that today in the era of antibiotics

67
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:30,260
would have been easily cured.

68
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,560
The sudden death of George Washington

69
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:35,080
is one such example.

70
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:38,780
He'd had apparently a long day working on the farm

71
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:41,380
and developed a sore throat.

72
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,680
Had difficulty speaking, so might be some sort of laryngitis.

73
00:04:45,681 --> 00:04:51,120
The doctors were called, and they blistered his throat.

74
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:55,320
They gave him some gargles of vinegar.

75
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:58,720
Then they let, I think it was three pints of blood,

76
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:04,800
from his arm and gave him various other remedies.

77
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:09,740
And he died within 48 hours.

78
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:13,040
In the late 19th and early 20th centuries,

79
00:05:13,041 --> 00:05:17,280
tuberculosis was one of the most dreaded infectious diseases,

80
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:21,120
killing many people in their prime.

81
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:26,180
If you were fairly wealthy, you might go to the mountains

82
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:31,260
and you would be given good food, a lot of fresh air.

83
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,700
Probably you would be put outside to have freeze to death

84
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,700
on the grounds that fresh air was good for you.

85
00:05:38,701 --> 00:05:42,240
I was good for you.

86
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:45,740
These treatments, however, were often ineffective.

87
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:48,780
Fortune and fame did little to save many

88
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:53,820
from meeting untimely deaths, including the poet John Keats

89
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:57,320
and the composer Frederic Chopin, both of whom

90
00:05:57,420 --> 00:05:59,720
expired before their 40th birthdays.

91
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:12,040
Death was an accepted part of daily life.

92
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:15,800
Nearly every family lost one or two members,

93
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:19,880
often young children, to infectious disease.

94
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:24,280
Statistics indicate that nearly 25% of the babies born died

95
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:26,620
before their first birthday.

96
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:28,920
The course of an infectious disease

97
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:33,060
was usually swift and violent.

98
00:06:33,061 --> 00:06:37,800
swift and violent.

99
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,960
A tough infectious disease like scarlet fever

100
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:44,840
was a struggle, a battle, because essentially,

101
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:49,740
all the doctors could really do was to manage the illness.

102
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:53,420
And so you would try and alleviate the symptoms.

103
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,260
You would try and keep the fever down as much as possible.

104
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,680
But there would reach a point where,

105
00:07:00,681 --> 00:07:05,820
the war between the germs and the body's natural defenses,

106
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,180
where it seemed like the germs might be winning.

107
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:11,260
And this was the crisis period.

108
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:13,860
In a matter of weeks or even days,

109
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:17,060
it was possible for diseases like pneumonia and scarlet

110
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:22,140
fever to wipe out all the children in a household.

111
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:26,100
I think that must have been a very searing and very

112
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:29,480
different experience of life and death

113
00:07:29,481 --> 00:07:35,220
to be living so closely to the deaths of so many young people

114
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,720
and children.

115
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:41,100
Living with the knowledge that death was so close

116
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:45,260
shaped everyday life.

117
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:47,560
It did really affect the way that people

118
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:52,740
planned their families and thought about their children.

119
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:56,100
This is not to say that people didn't love their children.

120
00:07:56,101 --> 00:07:59,740
People have always loved their children.

121
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:05,440
But people were a lot more inured to the idea

122
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:11,440
of maybe losing one or two, because that was just a sort

123
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:12,860
of natural thing.

124
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:18,420
It was not until the late 19th century

125
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:22,560
that man began to understand the causes of disease.

126
00:08:22,561 --> 00:08:25,660
It was to be the dawning of a remarkable new era

127
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:27,000
in medical science.

128
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:39,780
In the year 1901, 20-year-old Alexander Fleming

129
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:44,020
began medical school at St. Mary's College in London.

130
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:46,320
The atmosphere in which he found himself

131
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:49,360
was alive with creative discussion,

132
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:51,860
for more had occurred to change medicine

133
00:08:51,861 --> 00:08:55,860
in the last two decades than in the previous 2,000 years.

134
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:03,300
The course of medical science had been forever transformed

135
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:07,200
by the French microbiologist Louis Pasteur.

136
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:10,720
Working in Paris in the 1870s, Pasteur

137
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:14,520
had examined microorganisms under the microscope

138
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:18,620
and formulated what he called the germ theory of disease.

139
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:25,760
The germ theory was the revolutionary new idea

140
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:30,600
that diseases were living biological organisms that

141
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:33,100
came from outside the body.

142
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:39,080
For the first time, bacteria was seen as the cause of disease.

143
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:42,220
Pasteur, however, did more than lay the groundwork

144
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:45,120
for understanding the basis of disease.

145
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:49,020
He also raised the possibility that the human body could

146
00:09:49,021 --> 00:09:52,320
could be taught to destroy the germs that invaded it.

147
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:57,900
His experiments with the anthrax microbe

148
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,300
proved that weakened forms of that disease

149
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:03,640
could be injected back into cattle to help them

150
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:06,100
build an immunity against the bacteria.

151
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:12,640
It was not long before the German bacteriologist Robert

152
00:10:12,740 --> 00:10:15,540
Koch took the next step and developed

153
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:18,280
the technical procedures needed to classify

154
00:10:18,281 --> 00:10:19,780
to classify bacteria.

155
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:26,820
Once you isolated a pathogen from a human,

156
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:30,420
and you thought it was the agent that caused the disease,

157
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:31,620
you had to prove that.

158
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:35,200
So you had to re-inoculate that into another human,

159
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:37,200
or better, an experimental animal,

160
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:39,460
and show that it came down with the same disease.

161
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:43,640
That's part of what's known as Koch's postulates.

162
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:46,280
These advances in scientific knowledge

163
00:10:46,281 --> 00:10:48,620
available to the young Alexander Fleming

164
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:53,120
during his course of study at St. Mary's.

165
00:10:53,220 --> 00:10:56,920
He was also aware that there existed two very different

166
00:10:57,020 --> 00:10:59,920
approaches toward the treatment of infectious disease.

167
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:06,400
One approach had been pioneered by the German-born chemist,

168
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:08,700
Paul Ehrlich.

169
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,180
Working with the idea that synthetic chemicals

170
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:14,100
could be injected into the patient to target and kill

171
00:11:14,101 --> 00:11:18,680
and kill germs, Ehrlich developed the first effective treatment

172
00:11:18,681 --> 00:11:23,440
against syphilis, curing thousands.

173
00:11:23,441 --> 00:11:27,640
His magic bullet, however, was made out of an arsenic compound,

174
00:11:27,641 --> 00:11:31,380
which was painful when administered and sometimes

175
00:11:31,381 --> 00:11:32,220
lethal.

176
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:36,780
The other method of attacking disease

177
00:11:36,781 --> 00:11:39,720
was to develop vaccines to stimulate the body

178
00:11:39,721 --> 00:11:46,100
to produce its own natural defenses against bacteria.

179
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:47,960
The main proponent of this approach

180
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:52,100
was none other than Fleming's own professor at St. Mary's,

181
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:53,440
Sir Almroth Wright.

182
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:59,800
In 1906, Professor Wright invited Fleming

183
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:02,680
to join St. Mary's Pathology Department,

184
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:07,140
and Fleming became one of a select group of bacteriologists

185
00:12:07,141 --> 00:12:09,880
working to perfect a typhoid vaccine.

186
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:24,760
At the outbreak of World War I in 1914,

187
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:28,060
Alexander Fleming went to France as part of a battalion

188
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:31,800
of medical officers recruited to inoculate soldiers

189
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:35,660
against typhoid and study war wounds.

190
00:12:35,661 --> 00:12:38,260
He worked in a research center and surgical unit

191
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:41,960
at the casino in Boulogne, within earshot of the battlefield.

192
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:48,780
His experience with trench warfare

193
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:51,780
quickly taught him that medical treatment for the wounded

194
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:55,480
was primitive at best.

195
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:58,320
They would take them back to the hospitals.

196
00:12:58,420 --> 00:13:00,360
At least the field hospitals were probably tents

197
00:13:00,460 --> 00:13:04,080
or maybe just a stretcher at the side of the road.

198
00:13:04,081 --> 00:13:07,080
The wards, again, were nothing like hospitals

199
00:13:07,180 --> 00:13:09,320
that we would recognize.

200
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:14,460
And the hospitals were these large, open-air, open places,

201
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:16,700
although they tried to keep them clean, I think.

202
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:20,260
And cleanliness was probably next to godliness in that,

203
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:22,360
because there was little else they could do.

204
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:31,680
The fear for everyone was that an infection

205
00:13:31,681 --> 00:13:34,020
would develop in the wound.

206
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:37,560
And Fleming soon realized that because the fighting was taking

207
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:41,560
place on farmer's fields, which were highly fertilized and

208
00:13:41,660 --> 00:13:44,600
contained a rich flora of bacteria,

209
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:47,460
battle wounds were almost certain to become infected.

210
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:53,540
Unfortunately, only crude methods

211
00:13:53,640 --> 00:13:57,600
were available with which to sterilize the wounds.

212
00:13:57,601 --> 00:14:04,500
They tried to destroy the germs by using strong antiseptics,

213
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:08,840
carbolic acid or gentian violet.

214
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:12,020
The problem with these were that they often

215
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:16,880
did more damage to the white blood cells that

216
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:19,780
were trying to fight the infection than they

217
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:20,920
did to the bacteria.

218
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:27,600
If the doctors failed to prevent infection,

219
00:14:27,601 --> 00:14:30,240
They were essentially helpless.

220
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:33,040
Gangrene would set in, and their only recourse

221
00:14:33,140 --> 00:14:37,340
was to begin amputating.

222
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:40,880
They would start out maybe amputating the finger

223
00:14:40,980 --> 00:14:43,120
and then be forced to amputate at the wrist

224
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:46,820
and then move up to the elbow and then move up the arm

225
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:51,320
in this horrible and essentially fruitless effort

226
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:55,460
to stop the infection from spreading throughout the body.

227
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:00,700
Once an infection began to spread to the bloodstream,

228
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:06,580
the process of dying was slow, painful, and inescapable.

229
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:10,880
Hospital wards reeked of the stench of putrefying flesh.

230
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:14,360
The general opinion is that more soldiers

231
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:16,320
died of infections to their war wounds

232
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:20,360
than actually from the trauma of their wounds.

233
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:23,000
It was while he was working in the surgical ward

234
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:26,300
that Fleming conducted a series of experiments,

235
00:15:26,301 --> 00:15:29,480
which demonstrated that antiseptic chemicals, when

236
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:32,280
wrongly used, could do more harm than good.

237
00:15:36,380 --> 00:15:39,180
He was later to say that his experiences in Boulogne

238
00:15:39,280 --> 00:15:41,980
had shown him, as nothing else could,

239
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:44,680
the ravages of bacterial infection.

240
00:15:47,620 --> 00:15:53,820
He was devoted to controlling infections, burns, wounds,

241
00:15:53,821 --> 00:15:56,720
all of his scientific life.

242
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:02,260
And I imagine that that must have come from the horrifying

243
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:07,560
experience of watching hundreds of young soldiers lined up

244
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:11,300
in these military hospitals with shrapnel wounds

245
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:14,940
and other wounds that could not be properly dealt with,

246
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:18,620
could not be effectively cured.

247
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,420
After the war, Fleming returned

248
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:23,020
to London to the Department of Inoculation

249
00:16:23,021 --> 00:16:26,120
at St. Mary's to continue his research.

250
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:32,500
It was here in 1921 that he discovered lysozyme,

251
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:36,660
an enzyme in the human body that is secreted in tears

252
00:16:36,760 --> 00:16:39,340
and has the power to inhibit bacteria.

253
00:16:42,700 --> 00:16:44,640
For the next seven years, Fleming

254
00:16:44,740 --> 00:16:49,280
experimented with lysozyme, but his work led nowhere.

255
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:52,580
However, he continued to believe that a chemical

256
00:16:52,581 --> 00:16:55,880
substance might exist that could kill bacteria

257
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:58,820
without harming the body's own cells.

258
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:08,760
Then, in August of 1928, a serendipitous event

259
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:10,560
proved him right.

260
00:17:10,660 --> 00:17:13,900
Through sheer luck and a flash of genius,

261
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,240
Alexander Fleming made what has been called the most momentous

262
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:19,940
medical discovery of the 20th century.

263
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:27,580
For months, Fleming had been growing colonies of Staphylococci

264
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:32,580
bacteria on agar culture plates, trying to find out how individual

265
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:34,620
bacteria differ from one another.

266
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:43,640
Preparing to take his annual summer vacation,

267
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:46,580
he stored these culture plates on a workbench

268
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:49,080
while he went off for two weeks in the country.

269
00:17:49,081 --> 00:17:52,220
in the country.

270
00:17:52,320 --> 00:17:55,280
He returned to find the plates where he had left them.

271
00:17:55,380 --> 00:17:57,420
But on closer inspection, he noticed

272
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:01,220
that one of the cultures had become contaminated by a mold

273
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:05,900
and now had an unusual appearance.

274
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:11,040
The individual bacterial cells had burst.

275
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:15,280
And they were just ghost-like materials there.

276
00:18:15,281 --> 00:18:20,220
He saw this and immediately recognized what had happened.

277
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:23,620
He immediately recognized that the mold had put something

278
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:28,760
out because there was a gradient.

279
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:32,000
That is, closer to the mold, there was a greater effect.

280
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:33,500
And as you went away from the mold,

281
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:37,200
there was less of an effect.

282
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:39,540
Fleming later identified the spores

283
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:42,900
as penicillin, a common household mold.

284
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:49,120
He saw that around the mold, the colonies of Staphylococci

285
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:53,180
had become transparent as the penicillin had eaten away

286
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:54,920
their cell walls.

287
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:58,220
Farther out from the mold, the colonies of Staphylococci

288
00:18:58,320 --> 00:18:59,220
appeared normal.

289
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:07,140
A less acute observer might not have recognized the significance

290
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:09,140
of what he was seeing.

291
00:19:09,240 --> 00:19:13,580
Fleming, however, was immediately elated.

292
00:19:13,581 --> 00:19:16,620
Fleming at that moment represented what Pasteur

293
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:18,820
called the prepared mind.

294
00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:21,320
That is, he was prepared for this.

295
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:25,260
Everything he had done before this,

296
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,060
in terms of his research and what he was interested in, what

297
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:34,800
he was looking for, primed him for recognizing this.

298
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:37,200
Over the course of the next 18 months,

299
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:40,200
Fleming tested penicillin on many bacteria

300
00:19:40,201 --> 00:19:44,440
and worked out different methods for culturing the mold.

301
00:19:44,540 --> 00:19:46,440
His natural next step should have

302
00:19:46,540 --> 00:19:49,180
been to produce large amounts of the substance

303
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:52,280
and test it on laboratory animals.

304
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:54,820
But for Fleming, this proved impossible.

305
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:00,380
Fleming found penicillin difficult to work with

306
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:04,300
because it lost its effectiveness as an antibacterial agent

307
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:06,200
within hours.

308
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:09,320
When he asked St. Mary's to put up money for chemical research

309
00:20:09,321 --> 00:20:12,960
so he could try to purify the substance, he was turned down.

310
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:19,560
And although he went on to publish two papers

311
00:20:19,660 --> 00:20:22,300
documenting penicillin's remarkable ability

312
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:27,180
to kill bacteria, his discovery was completely ignored

313
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:29,880
by the scientific community.

314
00:20:29,980 --> 00:20:32,340
Flemming was a very taciturn Scots.

315
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:33,540
He wasn't very outgoing.

316
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:35,840
He wasn't very gregarious.

317
00:20:35,940 --> 00:20:37,940
And he did science by himself.

318
00:20:37,941 --> 00:20:40,940
He also was a very poor speaker.

319
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:44,680
And in those days, scientists were not the entrepreneurs

320
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:49,480
that some of them are today.

321
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:52,460
After two frustrating years of trying

322
00:20:52,560 --> 00:20:56,520
to extract a pure form of penicillin from the mold,

323
00:20:56,620 --> 00:20:58,360
Fleming gave up.

324
00:20:58,460 --> 00:21:02,700
He was forced to shrug off penicillin as another failure.

325
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:06,560
And for the next decade, the potential of this miracle drug

326
00:21:06,561 --> 00:21:08,060
went completely unnoticed.

327
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:21,240
In 1938, the specter of another global conflict

328
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:27,120
hung over Europe, rekindling memories of the last war.

329
00:21:27,220 --> 00:21:29,020
It had people in the War Department

330
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:35,180
worried because they had seen a generation of young men

331
00:21:35,181 --> 00:21:39,180
killed or disabled in World War I.

332
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:40,420
And here it was.

333
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:42,280
It was barely 20 years later.

334
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:46,720
And the sons of those people were likely to be facing

335
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:51,200
conditions that were perhaps even more horrific

336
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:52,200
on the battlefield.

337
00:21:57,600 --> 00:21:59,580
Among the numerous scientists hoping

338
00:21:59,680 --> 00:22:02,840
to find a cure for infection in war wounds

339
00:22:02,841 --> 00:22:07,780
was a young professor at Oxford named Howard Florey.

340
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:11,320
Realizing the need to combine his knowledge of bacteriology

341
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:14,320
with chemical skill, he recruited the help

342
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:17,960
of a brilliant chemist, Ernst Cheyne, who had recently

343
00:22:18,060 --> 00:22:19,760
escaped Hitler's Germany.

344
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:27,500
Cheyne began hunting through the published results

345
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:31,260
of past experiments looking for incomplete work that

346
00:22:31,261 --> 00:22:34,300
that was worth pushing forward.

347
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:39,460
He soon discovered Fleming's papers on penicillin.

348
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:42,640
Using the newly developed method of freeze drying,

349
00:22:42,740 --> 00:22:45,540
Ernst Chain managed to do what Fleming had never

350
00:22:45,640 --> 00:22:49,440
been able to accomplish, extract the active ingredient

351
00:22:49,540 --> 00:22:54,880
from the penicillin mold and purify it.

352
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:57,580
With an injectable form of penicillin in hand,

353
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:00,560
Flory and Chain were now ready to take the substance

354
00:23:00,561 --> 00:23:05,300
to the next step, experimentation on laboratory animals.

355
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:16,940
In September of 1939, three days after Britain

356
00:23:17,040 --> 00:23:20,580
had declared war on Germany, Howard Florey asked

357
00:23:20,680 --> 00:23:23,080
for a government grant to develop penicillin.

358
00:23:25,860 --> 00:23:27,960
There could hardly have been a worse time

359
00:23:27,961 --> 00:23:31,660
to launch a research project, as the whole of the country

360
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:33,260
was disrupted by war.

361
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:42,600
Flory was awarded a mere 50 pounds, the equivalent of $234.

362
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:46,120
The money was barely enough to pay his lab's clerical costs.

363
00:23:57,960 --> 00:24:02,140
In May of 1940, while the entire British expeditionary force

364
00:24:02,240 --> 00:24:05,860
was being driven out of Dunkirk by the German army,

365
00:24:05,960 --> 00:24:10,580
Florey prepared eight white mice for an experiment.

366
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:16,820
He injected all eight mice with streptococci, a lethal germ.

367
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:20,720
One group of four was returned to its cage,

368
00:24:20,820 --> 00:24:24,860
and a second group of four was inoculated with penicillin.

369
00:24:24,861 --> 00:24:28,800
was inoculated with penicillin.

370
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:32,160
And that was the experiment that basically the world

371
00:24:32,260 --> 00:24:37,500
was waiting for Fleming to have done in 1928.

372
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:41,240
By the next day, the first group of mice were dead,

373
00:24:41,340 --> 00:24:43,840
while the mice that had received the penicillin

374
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:45,540
played in their cage.

375
00:24:45,640 --> 00:24:50,520
I think they were very, very excited and somewhat astounded,

376
00:24:50,620 --> 00:24:54,860
because I don't think they knew how effective this was.

377
00:24:54,861 --> 00:24:58,360
material was going to be or was.

378
00:24:58,460 --> 00:25:02,600
And I think it was very, very shortly afterwards

379
00:25:02,700 --> 00:25:06,300
that they thought of trying this on patients.

380
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:12,620
Culturing enough mold necessitated

381
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:16,920
growing it in everything, from dishes to hospital bedpans.

382
00:25:17,020 --> 00:25:20,220
It proved to be an arduous process.

383
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:22,520
The first patient that they tried it on

384
00:25:22,521 --> 00:25:25,320
was somebody who was virtually at death's door,

385
00:25:25,420 --> 00:25:30,660
a London policeman who had developed a systemic infection

386
00:25:30,760 --> 00:25:32,460
from a thorn scratch.

387
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:35,060
He was working at his rose garden,

388
00:25:35,160 --> 00:25:38,060
and he had scratched his cheek.

389
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:41,480
Now, think of that today, scratching your face with a thorn

390
00:25:41,580 --> 00:25:45,980
and dying of or being near death because of the infection.

391
00:25:46,080 --> 00:25:48,380
I mean, it's unheard of.

392
00:25:48,480 --> 00:25:52,520
And they gave him penicillin.

393
00:25:52,521 --> 00:25:56,220
And he virtually rose from the dead.

394
00:25:56,320 --> 00:25:58,460
Day by day, he got better and better.

395
00:25:58,560 --> 00:26:03,100
And as he got better, they were running out of penicillin.

396
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:07,100
Florian Chain had only a limited amount of penicillin.

397
00:26:07,200 --> 00:26:09,400
And unfortunately, it wasn't enough

398
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:12,340
to save the policeman's life.

399
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:14,880
It was now clear that a full course of therapy

400
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:20,380
required 3,000 times the dose needed for a mouse.

401
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:22,120
For their next round of experiments,

402
00:26:22,121 --> 00:26:26,560
Flory chose six patients, five of them pediatric cases,

403
00:26:26,660 --> 00:26:31,900
thinking that children would not require as much medication.

404
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:35,640
And this was a miracle drug, because these people were

405
00:26:35,740 --> 00:26:38,100
destined to succumb.

406
00:26:38,200 --> 00:26:42,980
There was no way that the doctors at that time

407
00:26:43,080 --> 00:26:44,440
could save those people.

408
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:48,940
They picked the worst cases of the people who really needed it.

409
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:50,820
Penicillin killed the infection

410
00:26:50,821 --> 00:26:56,120
in all six cases, convincing Howard Florey of its value.

411
00:26:56,220 --> 00:26:59,300
As he watched bombs rain down on the city of London,

412
00:26:59,400 --> 00:27:03,440
he knew that he could not afford to waste any more time.

413
00:27:03,540 --> 00:27:08,600
He must look outside England for financial support.

414
00:27:08,700 --> 00:27:11,940
In the winter of 1941, Florey made the decision

415
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:16,020
to appeal to the United States and the Rockefeller Foundation,

416
00:27:16,120 --> 00:27:18,120
from whom he had once won a fellowship.

417
00:27:20,820 --> 00:27:23,460
Florey chose a colleague named Norman Heatley

418
00:27:23,560 --> 00:27:26,960
to accompany him to America.

419
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:30,060
Leaving Ernst Chain behind to oversee their production

420
00:27:30,160 --> 00:27:33,460
efforts in June of 1941, Florey and Heatley

421
00:27:33,560 --> 00:27:34,960
boarded a Pan Am Clipper.

422
00:27:38,980 --> 00:27:41,680
They flew out of England in an airplane that

423
00:27:41,780 --> 00:27:43,580
had all its windows blacked out.

424
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:47,280
Well, it was because of the German Air Force.

425
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:49,480
They couldn't tell anybody where they were going.

426
00:27:49,481 --> 00:27:52,420
They flew to Portugal, to Lisbon.

427
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:57,960
And Lisbon was a hotbed of spies from both sides

428
00:27:58,060 --> 00:28:00,920
because it was a neutral country.

429
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:04,460
Flory and Heatley took a highly unusual precaution

430
00:28:04,560 --> 00:28:08,460
with the penicillin they carried with them.

431
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:14,880
They rubbed the spores of the mold into their clothing

432
00:28:14,881 --> 00:28:19,760
so that if they were captured and the mold was taken away

433
00:28:19,860 --> 00:28:22,760
from them, that somehow one of them

434
00:28:22,860 --> 00:28:26,820
would be able to get through to the United States with the mold

435
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:33,300
hidden in their clothing and restart these cultures.

436
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:36,140
Flory and his fellow traveler were well aware

437
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:39,740
that the substance they carried was precious.

438
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:44,240
But even they did not grasp its astonishing potential.

439
00:28:44,241 --> 00:28:48,480
a potential that was about to transform the very nature

440
00:28:48,580 --> 00:28:49,880
of everyday life.

441
00:28:56,720 --> 00:29:00,480
Howard Flory's mission to find financial support for penicillin

442
00:29:00,580 --> 00:29:02,000
was successful.

443
00:29:02,100 --> 00:29:05,560
And in December of 1941, the production of penicillin

444
00:29:05,660 --> 00:29:08,960
began in America, out of reach of the bombs that

445
00:29:09,060 --> 00:29:12,360
were falling on Britain.

446
00:29:12,361 --> 00:29:14,120
The effort to produce penicillin

447
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:20,340
required the full cooperation of academia and industry.

448
00:29:20,440 --> 00:29:24,940
It was so clear by that time that the production of penicillin

449
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:27,580
needed to be as much of a priority

450
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:32,140
as the production of tanks and the production of ammunition

451
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:34,040
and the production of airplanes.

452
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:37,220
In many ways, it was like the Manhattan Project.

453
00:29:37,320 --> 00:29:41,360
There were literally dozens of laboratories

454
00:29:41,361 --> 00:29:42,600
involved in this.

455
00:29:42,700 --> 00:29:44,560
It was very secretive.

456
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:47,540
Documentation was typically classified

457
00:29:47,640 --> 00:29:48,940
as confidential or secret.

458
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:53,440
Among those recruited were companies

459
00:29:53,540 --> 00:29:57,140
focused on the manufacture of industrial chemicals,

460
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:00,540
including Merck, Squibb, and Pfizer.

461
00:30:00,640 --> 00:30:03,280
Pfizer turned out to be in the best position

462
00:30:03,380 --> 00:30:05,420
because of their extensive experience

463
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:09,920
producing substances like acetone and citric acid.

464
00:30:09,921 --> 00:30:13,660
through the process of deep tank fermentation.

465
00:30:13,760 --> 00:30:17,260
Imagine a glass of, a glass half full of water.

466
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:18,960
Well, there's a surface, and you can grow things

467
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:21,660
on the surface there, and the mold will grow very nicely

468
00:30:21,760 --> 00:30:23,860
because it needs oxygen.

469
00:30:23,960 --> 00:30:27,100
But there's the whole volume of the liquid that is far greater

470
00:30:27,200 --> 00:30:29,200
than the surface.

471
00:30:29,300 --> 00:30:32,280
If you can grow the mold in that, in the volume,

472
00:30:32,380 --> 00:30:36,080
you'd grow much, much more mold, and you'd get much, much more

473
00:30:36,081 --> 00:30:39,220
or a product, whether it's a vitamin or penicillin.

474
00:30:43,880 --> 00:30:48,860
Throughout 1942, penicillin remained a well-kept secret.

475
00:30:48,960 --> 00:30:51,520
Only a small quantity was available,

476
00:30:51,620 --> 00:30:54,360
and that was reserved entirely for military use.

477
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:03,300
It was not until later that year when a catastrophic fire raised

478
00:31:03,301 --> 00:31:08,540
coconut grove nightclub, leaving 400 victims severely burned

479
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:11,240
and vulnerable to infection.

480
00:31:11,340 --> 00:31:15,240
But the American public became aware of this miracle drug.

481
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:18,620
The drama of having this mystery drug brought in

482
00:31:18,720 --> 00:31:22,880
by a cavalcade of cars into Boston to treat these patients,

483
00:31:22,980 --> 00:31:25,760
a high profile burn brought penicillin

484
00:31:25,860 --> 00:31:28,020
to the minds of those decision makers

485
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:30,920
that this is a powerful drug.

486
00:31:30,921 --> 00:31:33,620
And of course, to the public, who then

487
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:36,420
began clamoring for this miracle drug.

488
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:44,540
By June of 1943, penicillin production in the United

489
00:31:44,640 --> 00:31:49,080
States had reached 425 million units per month,

490
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:53,440
enough to treat only 170 patients.

491
00:31:53,540 --> 00:31:57,480
By D-Day in June of 1944, the amount produced

492
00:31:57,580 --> 00:32:00,920
was enough for 40,000 cases.

493
00:32:00,921 --> 00:32:07,160
One year later, in 1945, Alexander Fleming, Ernst

494
00:32:07,260 --> 00:32:11,160
Cheyne, and Howard Florey were awarded the Nobel Prize

495
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:13,860
for medicine.

496
00:32:13,960 --> 00:32:17,540
The age of antibiotics had begun, and with it,

497
00:32:17,640 --> 00:32:19,800
the search for other miracle drugs.

498
00:32:25,820 --> 00:32:29,680
It was apparent from the early days using penicillin

499
00:32:29,681 --> 00:32:33,180
that one antibiotic wasn't going to be effective

500
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:35,080
for all infections.

501
00:32:35,180 --> 00:32:39,620
For one thing, penicillin has what we as clinicians called

502
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:43,520
a narrow spectrum of activity.

503
00:32:43,620 --> 00:32:46,360
While penicillin was active against bacteria

504
00:32:46,460 --> 00:32:49,640
like staphylococci and streptococci,

505
00:32:49,740 --> 00:32:53,440
it did not work against the tuberculosis bacilli.

506
00:32:53,540 --> 00:32:56,340
With this in mind, the pharmaceutical industry

507
00:32:56,440 --> 00:32:59,680
went out into the world to look for new and better

508
00:32:59,681 --> 00:33:01,720
agents.

509
00:33:01,820 --> 00:33:05,420
One bacteriologist at Rutgers, Selman Waxman,

510
00:33:05,520 --> 00:33:09,320
thought the answer might lie in the microorganisms

511
00:33:09,420 --> 00:33:12,820
that exist in soil.

512
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:15,400
He had found in these soil microorganisms

513
00:33:15,500 --> 00:33:17,660
this therapeutic antibiotic effect.

514
00:33:17,760 --> 00:33:21,660
So he began a very intense investigation

515
00:33:21,760 --> 00:33:26,600
of the streptomyces family and isolated one product

516
00:33:26,601 --> 00:33:31,240
called streptomycin that looked to be extremely active.

517
00:33:31,340 --> 00:33:35,400
And it was actually a clinical group at the Mayo Clinic

518
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:41,580
who discovered the impact of streptomycin on tuberculosis.

519
00:33:41,680 --> 00:33:44,480
Over the next 20 years, many potent

520
00:33:44,580 --> 00:33:47,280
antibiotics were discovered.

521
00:33:47,380 --> 00:33:51,760
There are something like 15 to 20 different structural

522
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:55,660
antibiotics, which then have their own little derivatives.

523
00:33:55,661 --> 00:33:58,160
So there are over 100 different kinds,

524
00:33:58,260 --> 00:34:00,600
but they all can be sort of put into little families.

525
00:34:03,500 --> 00:34:07,800
During the 1950s, antibiotics proliferated.

526
00:34:07,900 --> 00:34:11,500
Vast quantities were sold to the agricultural industry

527
00:34:11,600 --> 00:34:16,420
as a means of increasing the productivity of farm animals.

528
00:34:16,520 --> 00:34:20,360
I think one of the important features of antibiotics

529
00:34:20,460 --> 00:34:24,080
that should not be missed is that their discovery

530
00:34:24,081 --> 00:34:28,360
was not only a boon to humanity, but to pharma

531
00:34:28,460 --> 00:34:32,260
and pharmaceutical industries themselves.

532
00:34:32,360 --> 00:34:35,220
The unparalleled success of antibiotics

533
00:34:35,320 --> 00:34:39,160
became the impetus for the discovery of many new drugs,

534
00:34:39,260 --> 00:34:44,840
setting a tone for the 1960s and spawning a new trend,

535
00:34:44,940 --> 00:34:48,400
the celebrity fundraiser.

536
00:34:48,500 --> 00:34:51,580
I think antibiotics have given us

537
00:34:51,581 --> 00:34:59,580
a tremendous sense of optimism, of hubris over disease.

538
00:34:59,581 --> 00:35:02,560
The charity tourney has attracted some of the best

539
00:35:02,561 --> 00:35:05,560
loved names in show business, who always managed to swing

540
00:35:05,561 --> 00:35:08,560
a laugh above par.

541
00:35:08,561 --> 00:35:12,060
The war on cancer was just an extension

542
00:35:12,061 --> 00:35:16,100
that if you put a certain amount of money and effort

543
00:35:16,101 --> 00:35:19,580
at a problem, you can make it go away.

544
00:35:19,581 --> 00:35:24,120
And I think it was very much a sort of American can do,

545
00:35:24,220 --> 00:35:28,820
science and technology can take care of our problems attitude

546
00:35:28,920 --> 00:35:33,660
that gained a lot of its persuasiveness

547
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:38,200
from the immense power of antibiotics.

548
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:40,900
By the end of the decade, nearly one third

549
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:42,900
of the research and development budget

550
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,080
of the pharmaceutical industry was

551
00:35:45,081 --> 00:35:48,620
was devoted to developing new antibiotics

552
00:35:48,720 --> 00:35:52,120
at a cost of billions of dollars.

553
00:35:52,220 --> 00:35:56,460
The antibiotics that were discovered

554
00:35:56,560 --> 00:36:02,800
would control virtually every bacterial disease

555
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:08,700
that we faced then and that we are still facing now.

556
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:12,540
By the early 1970s, members of the medical profession

557
00:36:12,541 --> 00:36:15,540
and the American public believed the chapter

558
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:18,880
on bacterial infections was all but closed.

559
00:36:21,540 --> 00:36:26,080
So there was optimism, euphoria.

560
00:36:26,180 --> 00:36:35,560
We have really won this one war against the bacterial invaders.

561
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:41,100
And it was this false impression that we were secure with what

562
00:36:41,101 --> 00:36:47,100
with what we had that led to the often cited statement

563
00:36:47,200 --> 00:36:50,840
by the then FDA commissioner that we've won the war

564
00:36:50,940 --> 00:36:51,840
on infectious diseases.

565
00:36:54,840 --> 00:36:57,180
A feeling of complacency settled over

566
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:00,420
the pharmaceutical industry, and many companies shelved

567
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:05,320
further research and development on new antibiotics.

568
00:37:05,420 --> 00:37:08,420
Few pharmaceutical firms realized that the age

569
00:37:08,421 --> 00:37:13,560
of drug-resistant bacteria was already upon us,

570
00:37:13,561 --> 00:37:17,060
threatening to destroy the miracle of antibiotics.

571
00:37:22,360 --> 00:37:26,080
Drug-resistant bacteria emerged as a phenomenon

572
00:37:26,081 --> 00:37:29,800
as early as the 1940s in hospitals where penicillin

573
00:37:29,801 --> 00:37:32,340
was being used.

574
00:37:32,341 --> 00:37:36,580
At the time, few paid it much attention.

575
00:37:36,581 --> 00:37:41,360
Fleming himself warned in a New York Times interview

576
00:37:41,361 --> 00:37:46,460
that he was concerned that when the drug became orally available,

577
00:37:46,461 --> 00:37:51,060
that it would be so misused that bacteria would come back

578
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:53,460
no longer touchable by it.

579
00:37:53,461 --> 00:37:56,860
So he was clairvoyant in that way,

580
00:37:56,861 --> 00:38:01,040
as I think he was in the discovery.

581
00:38:01,041 --> 00:38:04,240
Other scientists were not so prescient.

582
00:38:04,241 --> 00:38:08,040
they continued to view antibiotics as invincible,

583
00:38:08,041 --> 00:38:12,280
ignoring evidence that bacteria were incredibly adoptable

584
00:38:12,281 --> 00:38:14,080
and would mutate in order to survive.

585
00:38:17,920 --> 00:38:20,900
When bacteria are exposed to an antibiotic,

586
00:38:20,901 --> 00:38:23,700
bacterial cells that are susceptible to the drug

587
00:38:23,701 --> 00:38:29,000
die, while those that are less sensitive to the drug survive.

588
00:38:31,100 --> 00:38:32,900
And if the amount of drug delivered

589
00:38:32,901 --> 00:38:37,400
to kill every last cell, the survivors emerge

590
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:42,580
with a new set of drug-resistant genetic traits.

591
00:38:42,680 --> 00:38:45,240
These drug-resistant bacteria are then

592
00:38:45,340 --> 00:38:49,780
able to exchange their genes with other bacteria,

593
00:38:49,880 --> 00:38:53,380
thus generating a whole new population

594
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:56,120
of highly resistant bacteria.

595
00:38:56,220 --> 00:38:58,120
They were here millions of years

596
00:38:58,220 --> 00:39:01,900
before the larger creatures.

597
00:39:01,901 --> 00:39:07,500
They have a survival repertoire, which is unbelievable

598
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:08,900
compared to what we have.

599
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:10,700
We have a life, we have a death, that's it.

600
00:39:10,800 --> 00:39:11,740
They go on.

601
00:39:11,840 --> 00:39:14,340
They create and recreate and create,

602
00:39:14,440 --> 00:39:16,140
and they're going to be here forever.

603
00:39:18,960 --> 00:39:22,060
Physicians prescribed medication in instances

604
00:39:22,160 --> 00:39:24,260
in which it was not needed.

605
00:39:24,360 --> 00:39:26,700
And the agricultural and dairy industries

606
00:39:26,800 --> 00:39:31,900
disseminated antibiotics with reckless abandon.

607
00:39:31,901 --> 00:39:35,100
By the 1980s, the scientific community

608
00:39:35,200 --> 00:39:39,100
could no longer ignore the problem.

609
00:39:39,200 --> 00:39:42,140
It had become apparent that drug-resistant bacteria

610
00:39:42,240 --> 00:39:46,520
were jumping from one individual to another.

611
00:39:46,620 --> 00:39:49,280
You step back and say, well, my heavens,

612
00:39:49,380 --> 00:39:54,260
the environment is contaminated with antibiotics.

613
00:39:54,360 --> 00:39:58,120
It's as if we poisoned the bacteria that were out there

614
00:39:58,220 --> 00:39:59,660
before.

615
00:39:59,661 --> 00:40:03,160
that were out there before, the susceptible ones,

616
00:40:03,260 --> 00:40:06,760
and they'd been replaced with the only ones that could survive,

617
00:40:06,860 --> 00:40:10,000
those that were resistant.

618
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:12,400
By the beginning of the 1990s,

619
00:40:12,500 --> 00:40:14,780
medical scientists recognized the need

620
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:18,640
to call public attention to the problem.

621
00:40:18,740 --> 00:40:22,420
I describe the present day resistance

622
00:40:22,520 --> 00:40:25,420
as a public health crisis.

623
00:40:25,520 --> 00:40:29,660
I cannot turn to my patients and assure them

624
00:40:29,661 --> 00:40:31,760
that if they catch an infection, that we're

625
00:40:31,860 --> 00:40:33,700
going to have an antibiotic that we'll

626
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:36,640
be able to treat them with.

627
00:40:36,740 --> 00:40:39,640
Health professionals labeled the 1990s

628
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:44,200
the decade of multi-drug resistance.

629
00:40:44,300 --> 00:40:48,140
It's the coming together of resistance traits

630
00:40:48,240 --> 00:40:53,920
in a single organism so that an infective agent will

631
00:40:54,020 --> 00:40:59,660
be insensitive to five, six, seven different antibiotics.

632
00:40:59,661 --> 00:41:02,860
That is an event of this decade, and it's everywhere.

633
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:08,540
Hospitals, communities, among animals, in plants.

634
00:41:08,640 --> 00:41:12,180
Physicians began to stress the need to use antibiotics

635
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:14,080
with great caution.

636
00:41:14,180 --> 00:41:16,180
I'm an infectious disease specialist,

637
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:19,380
and I spend most of my time telling people

638
00:41:19,480 --> 00:41:21,980
to cut back the number of antibiotics,

639
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:25,580
to not use them when they're not needed.

640
00:41:25,680 --> 00:41:28,220
We now find ourselves in a life and death

641
00:41:28,221 --> 00:41:30,960
with many of the same virulent organisms

642
00:41:31,060 --> 00:41:34,760
that were believed to have been conquered not that long ago.

643
00:41:36,860 --> 00:41:40,660
We now have strains of Streptococcus pneumonia

644
00:41:40,760 --> 00:41:43,360
that are resistant to penicillin.

645
00:41:43,460 --> 00:41:47,200
You cannot use that antibiotic with confidence

646
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:49,500
to treat that infection.

647
00:41:49,600 --> 00:41:51,340
And that is a big change.

648
00:41:51,440 --> 00:41:54,780
That's a very big change in practicing medicine.

649
00:41:54,880 --> 00:41:57,180
So I think physicians are alarmed,

650
00:41:57,181 --> 00:42:00,080
are alarmed and everybody is seeing it.

651
00:42:00,081 --> 00:42:04,520
Initially, it was just in scattered areas of the world,

652
00:42:04,521 --> 00:42:06,220
other parts of the United States,

653
00:42:06,221 --> 00:42:09,600
but not in your own backyard.

654
00:42:09,601 --> 00:42:13,260
Medical scientists believe that because all of us,

655
00:42:13,261 --> 00:42:15,540
patient, physician, and industry,

656
00:42:15,541 --> 00:42:18,240
have had a role in the problem, we also

657
00:42:18,241 --> 00:42:19,900
have a role in the solution.

658
00:42:23,400 --> 00:42:27,000
We are taking our message to the elementary and high

659
00:42:27,001 --> 00:42:30,600
kids, since they're the new parents, the new physicians,

660
00:42:30,700 --> 00:42:35,640
the new dentists, let them understand the role of antibiotics

661
00:42:35,740 --> 00:42:39,380
and that they are not the miracle drug.

662
00:42:39,480 --> 00:42:41,220
In the meantime, scientists have

663
00:42:41,320 --> 00:42:43,620
been working to find alternative methods

664
00:42:43,720 --> 00:42:46,220
of treating infection.

665
00:42:46,320 --> 00:42:49,460
One way is to develop protective vaccines.

666
00:42:49,560 --> 00:42:55,500
Another is to use bacteria to fight bacteria.

667
00:42:55,501 --> 00:42:59,740
There are other ways of reversing the resistance problem

668
00:42:59,840 --> 00:43:03,240
than trying to treat chemically.

669
00:43:03,340 --> 00:43:08,680
And that is to bring back in greater numbers

670
00:43:08,780 --> 00:43:11,980
the bacteria that you want there,

671
00:43:12,080 --> 00:43:15,380
to let bacteria fight it out with bacteria on an even playing

672
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:16,180
field.

673
00:43:16,280 --> 00:43:19,020
Don't give any antibiotics.

674
00:43:19,120 --> 00:43:20,960
In some countries around the world,

675
00:43:21,060 --> 00:43:24,000
doctors have heeded this advice.

676
00:43:24,001 --> 00:43:27,040
Finland is one such example.

677
00:43:27,140 --> 00:43:29,000
For decades, Finnish health professionals

678
00:43:29,100 --> 00:43:31,580
have followed a policy of restraint

679
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:35,440
in prescribing antibiotics, and as a result,

680
00:43:35,540 --> 00:43:38,620
report few problems with drug-resistant bacteria.

681
00:43:43,280 --> 00:43:47,020
Medical scientists remain hopeful that the prudent use

682
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:52,300
of antibiotics, coupled with new strategies to fight bacteria,

683
00:43:52,301 --> 00:43:57,600
can return us to that optimistic moment not so many years ago,

684
00:43:57,700 --> 00:44:01,740
when penicillin first saved a human life.

